<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Helian Xia</h1><p class="st-meta">407 CE – 431 CE · capital Tongwan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Xiongnu kingdom of Helian Bobo, builder of the white-walled city of Tongwan on the edge of the Ordos desert, which briefly took Chang'an from the Jin.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helian-bobo">Liu Bobo</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiefu">Tiefu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, was a commander under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a> who broke away in 407 and proclaimed himself Heavenly King of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-xia">Great Xia</a>, claiming descent from the ancient <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/xia-dynasty">Xia dynasty</a>. He later took the surname Helian.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helian-bobo">Helian Bobo's</a> mobile cavalry raided <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a> ruthlessly. Between 413 and 418 he built the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwan">Tongwan</a>, its walls of pounded earth mixed with lime so hard that, it was said, builders were executed if an awl could pierce them; its white ruins survive in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. In 418 he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a> forces left by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liu-song">Liu Yu</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helian-bobo">Helian Bobo's</a> death in 425, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwan">Tongwan</a> in 427 and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helian-chang">Helian Chang</a> the following year. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helian-ding">Helian Ding</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-qin">Western Qin</a> in 431 but was captured by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tuyuhun">Tuyuhun</a> while crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> and handed to the Wei.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/helian-xia.jpg" alt="Helian Xia" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_423_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>407 CE – 431 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tongwan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Helian Bobo, Helian Chang, Helian Ding</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xiongnu, Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hu Xia, Xia (Sixteen Kingdoms)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=419&amp;focus=helian-xia" aria-label="Helian Xia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7393 812 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Helian Xia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7713 1068L7616 1132M7713 1068L7769 1191M7713 1068L7496 1100"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7616 1132h0M7769 1191h0M7496 1100h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7713 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7713 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tongwan. <a href="/atlas?year=419&amp;focus=helian-xia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
